WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.709
Film Courage: Marc, when you were first creating
SPACE COMMAND, was it in your mind that you

00:00:03.709 --> 00:00:06.210
were going to attempt to sell it to a studio?

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:10.900
Marc Zicree: It’s very interesting, you
know…when I started in television…I’ve

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:14.010
been in a professional writer since I was
age 19.

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:18.169
I sold…well actually in truth…my first
radio play aired when I was 18 on [Los Angeles

00:00:18.169 --> 00:00:21.230
radio station] KPFK, my first short story
I sold when I was 19.

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:26.300
I started writing my first book when I was
22, the TWILIGHT ZONE COMPANION and I was

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:29.519
writing for television by the time I was 22
or 23.

00:00:29.519 --> 00:00:34.040
And when I started in television (and if you
wanted your work to reach millions of people),

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.310
it had to be through a studio or a network.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:41.480
And there were only 3 networks, ABC, NBC and
CBS…and also PBS, you know, public television.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:43.170
And that was it.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:49.860
So now…and also you had to shoot on 33mm
or 16 mm and you edited on a Moviola and it

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:54.630
cost millions of dollars to make any television
program per episode.

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:56.020
Now (of course) it’s very, very different.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:01:01.110
You can shoot on a digital camera (we own
a Red camera) and you edit on a laptop.

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:04.850
Our editor is back at our place right now
editing SPACE COMMAND on laptop and it’s

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:07.780
hooked up to our big screen TV.

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:08.900
And you can reach out to your audience.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:11.100
Your audience can help finance what you’re
doing.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:14.460
You can also reach millions of people via
the Internet.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:19.719
So a lot of people stay stuck in the way things
used to be and I think that’s a mistake.

00:01:19.719 --> 00:01:22.229
I embrace the new model and I love the new
model.

00:01:22.229 --> 00:01:24.420
And I embrace the direct communication with
my audience.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.850
I feel that I’m the same as my audience.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:30.950
So if I like something…if I feel enthusiastic
about something, I know my audience will be,

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:31.990
too.

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:36.229
And so with SPACE COMMAND, initially my idea
was…it’s funny.

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:41.759
When I first came up with the idea of doing
a hopeful view of the future, it’s a multi-generational

00:01:41.759 --> 00:01:43.859
saga that covers 150 years in the future.

00:01:43.859 --> 00:01:46.729
It takes place in the solar system and beyond.

00:01:46.729 --> 00:01:51.309
And a lot of my friends run network shows
and a lot of them said to me “Well, listen.

00:01:51.309 --> 00:01:54.649
We can team up and walk this into the network
and we can get a pilot deal.”

00:01:54.649 --> 00:02:00.360
But I new from experience that if I sold it
(and I knew that it probably would sell),

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.450
I’d be hired to write the script, then the
network would cut it off at the script and

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.850
then they would own it and no one would ever
see it.

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:09.319
Or they could green light the production to
pilot, and then we’d shoot the pilot and

00:02:09.319 --> 00:02:12.650
if we didn’t green light it to series (again)
no one would ever see it.

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:17.319
Or if they green lit it to series, they might
have notes that would screw it up and ruin

00:02:17.319 --> 00:02:18.319
it, so….[laughs].

00:02:18.319 --> 00:02:20.200
So I thought no “Well let me see if I can
raise money?”

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:24.190
I’d never raised money before, but I said
“Let’s see if I can reach out to the audience?”

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:29.000
I mentor thousands of young people in Hollywood
(well actually people of all ages) via the

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.010
roundtable I run [called THE TABLE] and the
classes I teach and I’d been hearing about

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:35.709
crowdfunding so I thought “Well let’s
see I can do this?”

00:02:35.709 --> 00:02:40.060
So the target was $75,000 to raise in 2 months.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:44.500
We hit that it in 3 days and we kept going
and we raised $221,000.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:48.019
And then I sold investment shares to my backers
and raised another half a million.

00:02:48.019 --> 00:02:49.690
And we’re still going strong.

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:53.400
And that was enough to open our own studio,
shoot the first 2 hours of SPACE COMMAND,

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:57.030
shoot 30 minutes of the second two hours,
I’ve written the first eight hours and outlined

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:58.270
hours 9 through 12.

00:02:58.270 --> 00:02:59.319
So that’s a season.

00:02:59.319 --> 00:03:03.159
So then the question is “Well do I want
to take it to a network?”

00:03:03.159 --> 00:03:06.260
Is there a reason to take it to a network
if you’ve gotten this much ahead of steam?

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:11.450
There are over 500 scripted comedies and dramas
of all the various networks and platforms

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:12.450
now.

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:13.660
500 series.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:18.819
The cost of rolling out a show and promoting
it (just so people know it exists) is $23

00:03:18.819 --> 00:03:19.959
million dollars.

00:03:19.959 --> 00:03:23.750
I can raise enough money to make SPACE COMMAND,
I can’t raise enough money to promote SPACE

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:24.750
COMMAND.

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:29.060
So that is where a studio or a network (or
more ideally, more specifically a network)

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:33.859
or a big platform like Netflix or Amazon makes
sense because if I really want to reach a

00:03:33.859 --> 00:03:38.230
world audience on a broad scale, if I really
want this to really last, and reach the people

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:39.950
I want to reach, that makes a lot of sense.

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:43.100
Elaine Zicree: And the fact of the matter
is he started out wondering if this would

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:45.319
be a single film.

00:03:45.319 --> 00:03:49.940
And what happened was once we started with
the crowdfunding, something that we hadn’t

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:55.480
seen as an aspect of crowdfunding, wasn’t
just gathering money to shoot something, but

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:58.640
it was gathering money for awareness worldwide.

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:02.109
And that awareness through us because we’re
both self-bound people and we thought maybe

00:04:02.109 --> 00:04:06.290
a few other people are really down on this
dark trend and will want to see something

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:09.150
that is hopeful, a hopeful vision of the future.

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:15.400
And in fact, the outpouring was so great,
that is what ignited this crazy ambition in

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:19.290
this mad person, and it started to grow and
grow and grow.

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:23.610
And we thought well maybe 2, maybe 3, and
so all it started as a single and it became

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:26.180
a much larger story.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:28.870
And it became “Well how about ongoingly?”

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:33.180
And we’ve had investors just walk up to
him and say “I invested in this because

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:36.360
I wanted to see something that gave me hope.”

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:37.370
Specifically.

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:41.580
And so we realized that something that was
troubling to us, was profoundly troubling

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:43.440
to vast numbers across the globe.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:48.480
Marc Zicree: Well it’s funny because during
our SPACE COMMAND campaign, I got a phone

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:49.480
call.

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:50.840
And it turns out that there is a real space
command.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:53.800
It’s the space going arm of the U.S. Air
Force.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:54.800
And they called me!

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:56.080
A Major in the Air Force called me.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:57.080
And I thought “Oh, gosh.

00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:59.770
There are either going to want to shut me
down or have me change the name.”

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:04.920
And I get on the phone with Major Glenn Roberts
of the U.S. Air Force and he says “We love

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:06.060
what you’re doing!

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:07.680
We want to be part of it.”

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:08.680
Great!

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:12.110
But the funny part is, during our (Kickstarter)
campaign if you type SPACE COMMAND into Google,

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:14.890
we came in first and the Air Force came in
second.

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:18.500
And even to this day, if you type in SPACE
COMMAND, the Air Force comes in first and

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:20.229
we come in second even still.

00:05:20.229 --> 00:05:23.120
So millions of people around the world know
about SPACE COMMAND.

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:26.639
Thousands gave us money, which is very, very
heartening.

00:05:26.639 --> 00:05:30.380
But as Elaine was saying, originally it was
going to be one 2-hour story and I actually

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:34.550
wrote half of it before the campaign and then
immediately sat down and wrote the second

00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:36.289
half of that 2-hour script.

00:05:36.289 --> 00:05:40.680
But because I was doing media interviews every
single day of the campaign, as I talked about

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:45.030
SPACE COMMAND, the story started to grow in
my mind and (as Elaine said) it grew from

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:49.610
2 hours, to 4 hours to 6 hours to 8 hours
and then suddenly it was a whole series.

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:54.330
And so when I sat down and what was originally
going to be the first 2 hours, ultimately

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:59.250
became the fourth story (the fourth 2-hour
story) and so I sat down with all the other

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:05.772
scripts…so I had written 4 of the 2-hour
scripts already and what was going to be the

00:06:05.772 --> 00:06:07.730
first one is now going to be the fourth one.

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:10.800
So if you ever go back to our original Kickstarter
campaign, the actors I’m talking about as

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:14.290
being in SPACE COMMAND actually now are in
that fourth one.

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:16.949
So it’s Armin Shimerman from STAR TREK:
DEEP SPACE NINE and BUFFY THE VAMPIRE SLAYER.

00:06:16.949 --> 00:06:20.710
Christina Moses who just starred in CONTAINMENT,
Ethan Phillips from STAR TREK: VOYAGER, etc.

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:25.349
Elaine Zicree: It’s sort of like a loose
tornado, it sweeps more and more people along…[laughs].

00:06:25.349 --> 00:06:28.680
Marc Zicree: Well the lovely thing is that
all of the actors I’ve approached (many

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:32.860
of them stars from shows I’ve worked on)
said “Yes” [to the project] and so …I

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:36.690
mean my friend Mike Harney from the show ORANGE
IS THE NEW BLACK and Doug Jones who starred

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:40.050
in PAN’S LABYRINTH and HELLBOY and THE STRAIN
and he’s going to be starring in the new

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:42.500
Star Trek TV show STAR TREK DISCOVERY.

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:48.139
And Mira Furlan from BABYLON 5 and LOST and
Bill Mumy from BABYLON 5 and LOST IN SPACE

00:06:48.139 --> 00:06:53.020
and Robert Picardo from STAR TREK VOYAGER
and STARGATE ATLANTIS, these are wonderful

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:54.020
actors.

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:56.960
James Hong who was in BLADE RUNNER and BIG
TROUBLE IN LITTLE CHINA (he’s one of my

00:06:56.960 --> 00:06:57.960
heroes).

00:06:57.960 --> 00:06:58.960
He’s phenomenal to work with.

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:00.250
And Faran Tahir who was in J.J.

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:02.990
Abrams STAR TREK movie and IRON MAN and ELYSIUM.

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:07.479
I mean these are phenomenal actors and I’m
honored to be working with them.

00:07:07.479 --> 00:07:08.479
It’s just such a joy.

00:07:08.479 --> 00:07:08.492
Question for the Viewers: How did you raise
money for your last project?

